<p>If April was about hardening the platform, May was about the agent stack starting to behave like normal infrastructure. The most important document of the month, for my work, was a protocol release candidate. It is not glamorous, but it changes how you deploy agents.</p>
<p>As with every briefing in this series, the platform and protocol items come from the projects' own posts, and I cite the coverage I used for regulation.</p>

<section class="block">
<h2>The month in one paragraph</h2>
<p>MCP published the release candidate for its 2026-07-28 specification, the largest revision since launch, with a stateless core, first-class extensions and tighter authorisation<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. OpenTelemetry became a CNCF graduated project<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup> and published a practical guide to GenAI observability<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>. In Brussels, the Parliament and Council reached a provisional agreement on the Digital Omnibus on AI<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>. Kubernetes spent the month publishing v1.36 deep dives on accelerators and AI scheduling<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-7">7</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-8">8</a></sup>.</p>
<table class="tbl">
<thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>What changed</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>MCP 2026-07-28 RC</td><td>Stateless core, extensions, auth hardening</td><td>Agents run behind ordinary load balancers</td></tr>
<tr><td>OpenTelemetry</td><td>CNCF graduation</td><td>Safe to standardise on, including for LLM telemetry</td></tr>
<tr><td>GenAI semantic conventions</td><td>Practical guide to LLM traces and metrics</td><td>Token cost and latency per call become visible</td></tr>
<tr><td>Digital Omnibus on AI</td><td>Provisional deal to delay high-risk deadlines</td><td>A planning date, not yet law</td></tr>
<tr><td>K8s DRA and scheduling</td><td>More drivers, gang scheduling evolves</td><td>Better GPU sharing for AI workloads</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>MCP: the session is gone</h2>
<p>The release candidate removes the <code>initialize</code> handshake and the <code>Mcp-Session-Id</code> header<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. Protocol version, client information and capabilities now travel with every request, and a new <code>server/discover</code> method returns server capabilities on demand. The post spells out the practical effect: a remote server that used to need sticky sessions, a shared session store and deep packet inspection at the gateway can now sit behind a plain round-robin load balancer, route on an <code>Mcp-Method</code> header, and let clients cache <code>tools/list</code>.</p>
<figure class="fig"><div class="im"><img src="/assets/img/briefings/mcp-stateless-topology.svg" alt="Before: a client routed with a sticky route to one MCP server instance, all instances sharing a session store. After: the client routed to any of three instances with no session store" width="960" height="420" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>Before and after: sticky routing and a shared session store give way to any-instance routing. <span class="credit">Source: <a href="https://blog.modelcontextprotocol.io/posts/2026-07-28-release-candidate/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MCP blog, The 2026-07-28 MCP Specification Release Candidate</a> · CC BY 4.0</span></figcaption></figure>
<p>Applications can still be stateful. The recommended pattern is an explicit handle: a tool returns an identifier such as a <code>basket_id</code>, and the model passes it back as an ordinary argument on later calls.</p>
<figure class="fig"><div class="im"><img src="/assets/img/briefings/mcp-handle-pattern.svg" alt="Sequence diagram: the model calls create_basket, the MCP server returns a basket_id, and the model passes the same basket_id to add_item" width="640" height="340" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>The explicit-handle pattern keeps state visible to the model instead of hidden in transport metadata. <span class="credit">Source: <a href="https://blog.modelcontextprotocol.io/posts/2026-07-28-release-candidate/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MCP blog, The 2026-07-28 MCP Specification Release Candidate</a> · CC BY 4.0</span></figcaption></figure>
<p>Two other changes matter for enterprise use. Extensions became first-class, with MCP Apps (server-rendered interfaces in a sandboxed iframe) and Tasks moving out of the core into an extension, which means anyone who built on the experimental Tasks API has a migration ahead. And six proposals hardened authorisation, including mandatory validation of the <code>iss</code> parameter per RFC 9207 to counter mix-up attacks<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. The final specification was scheduled for 28 July, with a ten-week window for SDKs to validate. I covered the security side in my <a href="/insights/securing-mcp-servers-checklist/">MCP security checklist</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Observability: OpenTelemetry graduates, and LLM calls get standard telemetry</h2>
<p>The CNCF announced OpenTelemetry's graduation on 21 May<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>. For architecture decisions, graduation is mostly about risk: it is now very hard to argue against OpenTelemetry as the default instrumentation standard.</p>
<figure class="fig"><div class="im"><img src="/assets/img/briefings/otel-genai-spans.webp" alt="A GenAI observability dashboard listing LLM call spans with model, duration and token information" width="1400" height="938" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>LLM and tool-call spans captured with the OpenTelemetry GenAI semantic conventions. <span class="credit">Source: <a href="https://opentelemetry.io/blog/2026/genai-observability/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenTelemetry blog, Inside the LLM Call: GenAI Observability with OpenTelemetry</a> · CC BY 4.0</span></figcaption></figure>
<p>The GenAI observability post is the one I would hand to any team running agents<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>. The semantic conventions standardise how a model call is recorded: the model, input and output token counts and, only when you opt in, the content of prompts, completions and tool calls. The default of not capturing content is the right one, because prompts often contain personal data. The post also notes that common coding assistants already emit OpenTelemetry data.</p>
<p>The project also launched Blueprints and Reference Implementations, an opinionated guide to deploying OpenTelemetry for common situations, prompted by users asking why adoption feels so complex<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>. I welcome it. Most of the complexity teams hit is accidental, not essential. A blueprint that says "do it this way" saves weeks of debate in a platform team.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Regulation: a provisional deal on the AI Act timetable</h2>
<p>On 7 May the Parliament and Council reached a provisional agreement on the Digital Omnibus on AI. As reported, it sets 2 December 2027 for stand-alone high-risk systems and 2 August 2028 for systems used as safety components of regulated products, adds a new prohibition on AI that generates child sexual abuse material or non-consensual intimate imagery, and still required formal adoption before the original 2 August 2026 date<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-6">6</a></sup>. In May this was a deal, not law. I explain what happened once it was adopted in <a href="/insights/eu-ai-act-digital-omnibus-roadmap/">the EU AI Act after the Digital Omnibus</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Kubernetes: getting serious about AI workloads</h2>
<p>The v1.36 deep dives were dominated by AI infrastructure. Dynamic Resource Allocation gained more drivers and features, extending to native resources such as CPU and memory and supporting ResourceClaims in PodGroups<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-7">7</a></sup>. Workload-aware scheduling, which handles gang scheduling for AI and batch jobs, took an architectural step by separating its API concerns<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-8">8</a></sup>. Declarative validation reached GA, replacing a large body of handwritten validation code with markers in the type definitions<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-9">9</a></sup>.</p>
<div class="callout"><p>One operational note: the Kubernetes Security Response Committee announced it would correct CVE records for a few older, unfixed issues on 1 June, warning that vulnerability scanners might start flagging them where they previously did not<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-10">10</a></sup>. If your scanner reports suddenly changed in June, this is a likely reason.</p></div>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>What I'd do with this</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Design new MCP servers stateless from day one.</strong> Use explicit handles for application state and keep sessions out of the transport.</li>
<li><strong>Put an AI gateway in front of agent traffic</strong> and route on the new MCP headers, so authentication, rate limits and logging live in one place.</li>
<li><strong>Instrument every LLM call with the GenAI conventions</strong>, metadata only by default, so you can report cost and latency per feature.</li>
<li><strong>Treat the AI Act dates as provisional</strong> until adoption, but keep building logging and evaluation regardless.</li>
<li><strong>If you schedule GPU work on Kubernetes</strong>, review DRA and gang scheduling before buying more accelerators. Better sharing is often cheaper than more hardware.</li>
</ol>
</section>

